@node	5.2, 5.3, 5.1, Chapter 5
@section A Register-Machine Simulator

In order to gain a good understanding of the design of register machines, we
must test the machines we design to see if they perform as expected.  One way
to test a design is to hand-simulate the operation of the controller, as in
@ref{Exercise 5.5}.  But this is extremely tedious for all but the simplest
machines.  In this section we construct a simulator for machines described in
the register-machine language.  The simulator is an OCaml module with four
interface operations.  The first uses a description of a register machine to
construct a model of the machine (a data structure whose parts correspond to
the parts of the machine to be simulated), and the other three allow us to
simulate the machine by manipulating the model:

@quotation

@example
(make-machine @var{register-names}
              @var{operations}
              @var{controller})
@end example

@noindent
constructs and returns a model of the machine with the given registers,
operations, and controller.

@example
(set-register-contents! @var{machine-model}
                        @var{register-name}
                        @var{value})
@end example

@noindent
stores a value in a simulated register in the given machine.

@example
(get-register-contents @var{machine-model}
                       @var{register-name})
@end example

@noindent
returns the contents of a simulated register in the given machine.

@example
(start @var{machine-model})
@end example

@noindent
simulates the execution of the given machine, starting from the beginning of
the controller sequence and stopping when it reaches the end of the sequence.
@end quotation

@noindent
As an example of how these procedures are used, we can define
@code{gcd-machine} to be a model of the @abbr{GCD} machine of
@ref{5.1.1} as follows:

@example ocaml
let gcd_machine =
  Machine.make_machine
    ~registers:[ "a"; "b"; "t" ]
    ~operations:Machine.arith_operations
    {|(controller
 test-b
   (test (op =) (reg b) (const 0))
   (branch (label gcd-done))
   (assign t (op rem) (reg a) (reg b))
   (assign a (reg b))
   (assign b (reg t))
   (goto (label test-b))
 gcd-done)|}
;;
(* = Ok <machine> -- the assembler checked every register, label, and
   operation before the machine exists *)
@end example

@noindent
The first argument to @code{make-machine} is a list of register names.  The
next argument is the operations table, pairing each operation name with the
host computation that implements the operation (that is, produces the same
output value given the same input values).  The last argument
specifies the controller as text in the book's notation, labels and machine
instructions, as in @ref{5.1}.

To compute @abbr{GCD}s with this machine, we set the input registers, start
the machine, and examine the result when the simulation terminates:

@example ocaml
gcd_machine >>= fun m ->
Machine.set_register m "a" (Machine.Int 206) >>= fun () ->
Machine.set_register m "b" (Machine.Int 40) >>= fun () ->
Machine.start m >>= fun () ->
Machine.get_register m "a"
;;
(* = Ok 2 -- each store answers [Ok ()], the book's done, and the
   stopped machine answers 2 *)
@end example

@noindent
This computation will run much more slowly than a @code{gcd} procedure written
in OCaml, because we will simulate low-level machine instructions, such as
@code{assign}, by much more complex operations.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.7}Exercise 5.7:} Use the simulator to test the
machines you designed in @ref{Exercise 5.4}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{Exercise 5.7a is added by this edition and extends exercise 5.7;
SICP numbers stop at 5.7.} @strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.7a}Exercise 5.7a:} Pair each simulated run
of Exercise 5.7 with a direct host computation of the same function as
an oracle: run the recursive Fibonacci machine of @ref{Figure 5.12}
and the iterative factorial machine of Exercise 5.1 on a shared set
of arguments, compute each answer directly on the host with the
corresponding procedure, and check that every simulated answer equals
its oracle's answer. State the oracle rule your demonstrations follow.
@end quotation

