@subsection The Machine Model

The machine model generated by @code{make-machine} is represented in this
edition as an abstract mutable record.  Where the book's model is a procedure
with local state that dispatches on messages, the techniques developed in
@ref{Chapter 3}, the edition's operations are the functions this section
defines over the record; the record's type stays abstract, so nothing outside
the module touches the parts directly.  To build this model,
@code{make-machine} begins by calling the procedure @code{make-new-machine} to
construct the parts of the machine model that are common to all register
machines.  This basic machine model constructed by @code{make-new-machine} is
essentially a container for some registers and a stack, together with an
execution mechanism that processes the controller instructions one by one.

@code{Make-machine} then extends this basic model (by operating on the record)
to include the registers, operations, and controller of the particular machine
being defined.  First it allocates a register in the new machine for each of
the supplied register names and installs the designated operations in the
machine.  Then it uses an @newterm{assembler} (described below in
@ref{5.2.2}) to transform the controller list into instructions for the new
machine and installs these as the machine's instruction sequence.
@code{Make-machine} returns as its value the modified machine model.

@example ocaml
let make_machine ~registers ~operations ~controller =
  let machine = make_new_machine () in
  seq (allocate_register machine) registers
  >>= fun () ->
  machine.ops := !(machine.ops) @ operations;
  assemble controller machine >>= fun () -> Ok machine
;;
@end example

@subsubheading Registers

The book represents a register as a procedure with local state, as in
@ref{Chapter 3}; the edition's register is a record with a mutable field,
which the same closures would wrap.  The procedure @code{make-register}
creates a register that holds a value that can be accessed or changed:

@example ocaml
type register = @{ name : string; mutable contents : value @}

let make_register name = @{ name; contents = Symbol "*unassigned*" @}
;;
@end example

@noindent
A register starts unassigned; the book's @code{*unassigned*} sentinel is the
symbol value here.  The name rides along for the register's own reports
(@ref{Exercise 5.18} traces by it).  The following procedures are used to
access registers:

@example ocaml
(* get-contents and set-contents! are the field itself:
   [reg.contents] reads, [reg.contents <- v] writes *)
@end example

@subsubheading The stack

We can also represent a stack as a procedure with local state.  The procedure
@code{make-stack} creates a stack whose local state consists of a list of the
items on the stack.  A stack accepts requests to @code{push} an item onto the
stack, to @code{pop} the top item off the stack and return it, and to
@code{initialize} the stack to empty.

@example ocaml
(* the stack of this subsection: entries with the three operations;
   5.2.4 hangs the monitoring counters off the same closures *)
let make_stack () =
  let entries = ref [] in
  let push v = entries := v :: !entries in
  let pop name =
    match !entries with
    | [] -> Error (Stack_underflow name)
    | top :: rest ->
      entries := rest;
      Ok top
  in
  let initialize () = entries := [] in
  (push, pop, initialize)
;;
@end example

@noindent
A pop names the register whose @code{restore} asked, so an empty stack is the
typed underflow failure of 5.1 rather than a raised error.  The following
procedures are used to access stacks:

@example ocaml
(* the book's [pop] and [push] wrappers are the closures themselves:
   [m.stack.pop name] and [m.stack.push v] *)
@end example

@subsubheading The basic machine

The @code{make-new-machine} procedure, shown in @ref{Figure 5.13,,@w{Figure 5.13}},
constructs
an object whose local state consists of a stack, an initially empty instruction
sequence, a list of operations that initially contains an operation to
initialize the stack, and a @newterm{register table} that initially contains
two registers, named @code{flag} and @code{pc} (for ``program counter'').  The
internal procedure @code{allocate-register} adds new entries to the register
table, and the internal procedure @code{lookup-register} looks up registers in
the table.  In this edition the @code{flag} register is an ordinary entry of
the table -- the branch reads it and fails with a typed error when no test has
yet set it -- while the @code{pc} is the machine's own index into the
instruction array, which labels assemble to; the record keeps the register
table and the array's side by side.

@noindent
@strong{@anchor{Figure 5.13}Figure 5.13:} @math{\downarrow} The @code{make-new-machine}
procedure, which implements the basic machine model.

@example ocaml
let make_new_machine () =
  let m =
    @{ regs = Hashtbl.create 16
    ; ops = ref []
    ; insts = [||]
    ; labels = Hashtbl.create 16
    ; stack = make_stack ()
    ; pc = ref 0
    ; output = ref [] @}
  in
  Hashtbl.replace m.regs "flag" (make_register "flag");
  let transcript line = m.output := !(m.output) @ [ line ] in
  m.ops :=
    [ ( "initialize-stack"
      , Action_op (fun _ ->
          m.stack.initialize ();
          Ok ()) ) ];
  m
;;

let allocate_register m name =
  if Hashtbl.mem m.regs name
  then Error (Bad_instruction ("the register " ^ name ^ " is declared twice"))
  else (
    Hashtbl.replace m.regs name (make_register name);
    Ok ())
;;

let register_of m name =
  match Hashtbl.find_opt m.regs name with
  | Some r -> Ok r
  | None -> Error (Unknown_register name)
;;
@end example

@noindent
The @code{flag} register is used to control branching in the simulated machine.
@code{Test} instructions set the contents of @code{flag} to the result of the
test (true or false).  @code{Branch} instructions decide whether or not to
branch by examining the contents of @code{flag}.

The @code{pc} register determines the sequencing of instructions as the machine
runs.  This sequencing is implemented by the driver loop @code{execute}.  In
the simulation model, each machine instruction is a data structure that
includes a procedure of no arguments, called the @newterm{instruction execution procedure},
such that calling this procedure simulates executing the
instruction.  As the simulation runs, @code{pc} points to the place in the
instruction sequence beginning with the next instruction to be executed.
@code{Execute} gets that instruction, executes it by calling the instruction
execution procedure, and repeats this cycle until there are no more
instructions to execute (i.e., until @code{pc} points to the end of the
instruction sequence, which the edition's index reaches when it counts up to
the array length).

@example ocaml
let start m =
  m.pc := 0;
  let rec execute () =
    if !(m.pc) >= Array.length m.insts
    then Ok ()
    else m.insts.(!(m.pc)).exec () >>= execute
  in
  execute ()
;;
@end example

@noindent
As part of its operation, each instruction execution procedure modifies
@code{pc} to indicate the next instruction to be executed.  @code{Branch} and
@code{goto} instructions change @code{pc} to point to the new destination.  All
other instructions simply advance @code{pc}, making it point to the next
instruction in the sequence.  Observe that each call to @code{execute} calls
@code{execute} again, but this does not produce an infinite loop because
running the instruction execution procedure changes the contents of @code{pc}.

@code{Make-new-machine} returns the record itself; where the book's
@code{dispatch} implements message-passing access to the internal state, the
edition's operations on the record are the functions of this section.
Notice that starting the machine
is accomplished by setting @code{pc} to the beginning of the instruction
sequence and calling @code{execute}.

For convenience, we provide the alternate procedural interface to a machine's
@code{start} operation, as well as procedures to set and examine register
contents, as specified at the beginning of @ref{5.2}:

@example ocaml
let set_register m name v =
  register_of m name >>= fun r ->
  r.contents <- v;
  Ok ()
;;

let get_register m name = register_of m name >>= fun r -> Ok r.contents
;;
@end example

@noindent
These procedures (and many procedures in @ref{5.2.2} and @ref{5.2.3})
use the following to look up the register with a given name in a given machine:

@example ocaml
(* the book's [get-register] is [register_of] above: it hands back
   the register record itself, the lookup the execution procedures
   capture at assembly time *)
@end example

