@subsection The Assembler

The assembler transforms the sequence of controller expressions for a machine
into a corresponding list of machine instructions, each with its execution
procedure.  Overall, the assembler is much like the evaluators we studied in
@ref{Chapter 4}---there is an input language (in this case, the
register-machine language) and we must perform an appropriate action for each
type of expression in the language.

The technique of producing an execution procedure for each instruction is just
what we used in @ref{4.1.7} to speed up the evaluator by separating
analysis from runtime execution.  As we saw in @ref{Chapter 4}, much useful
analysis of Scheme expressions could be performed without knowing the actual
values of variables.  Here, analogously, much useful analysis of
register-machine-language expressions can be performed without knowing the
actual contents of machine registers.  For example, we can replace references
to registers by pointers to the register objects, and we can replace references
to labels by pointers to the place in the instruction sequence that the label
designates -- in this edition, indices into the instruction array.

Before it can generate the instruction execution procedures, the assembler must
know what all the labels refer to, so it begins by scanning the controller text
to separate the labels from the instructions.  As it scans the text, it
constructs both a list of instructions and a table that associates each label
with a pointer into that list.  Then the assembler augments the instruction
list by inserting the execution procedure for each instruction.

The @code{assemble} procedure is the main entry to the assembler.  It takes the
controller text and the machine model as arguments and returns the instruction
sequence to be stored in the model.  @code{Assemble} calls the shared reader's
scan to build the initial instruction list and label table
from the supplied controller text, then uses @code{update-insts} to generate
the instruction execution procedures and insert them into the instruction list.

@example ocaml
let assemble controller m = parse_program controller >>= install_program m

let install_program (m : machine) (program : program) =
  check_registers m program
  >>= fun () ->
  let entries =
    List.map (fun (label, index) -> make_label_entry label index) program.labels
  in
  List.iter (fun (label, index) -> Hashtbl.replace m.labels label index) entries;
  update_insts program.code m >>= fun insts ->
  m.insts <- insts;
  Ok ()
;;
@end example

@noindent
The label scan is the shared reader's @code{parse_program}, the same reader the
machines of 5.1 load their controllers through.  It takes the controller text
and produces, first, the instruction data structures, each containing an
instruction from the text; and, second, the table @code{labels}, which
associates each label from the text with the position in the instruction
sequence that the label designates.

@example ocaml
(* the scan the reader performs: labels accumulate as they pass,
   and the result is the parsed program
   @{ code : instruction array; labels : (string * int) list @} -- a
   label at the end of the sequence names the stop index, one past
   the last instruction *)
let extract_labels controller = parse_program controller
;;
@end example

@noindent
The scan works by sequentially walking the elements of the text and
accumulating the instructions and the labels.  If an
element is a symbol (and thus a label) an appropriate entry is added to the
@code{labels} table.  Otherwise the element is parsed and accumulated onto the
instructions.@footnote{The book's scan passes a @code{receive} procedure to
itself, a way to get the scan to effectively return two values---@code{labels}
and the instructions---without explicitly making a compound data structure to
hold them.  An OCaml function returns the explicit pair directly, so this
edition's scan is the pair-returning alternative the text shows; the
continuation-passing shape itself is the backtracking control structure of the
@code{amb} evaluator in @ref{4.3.3}.}

@example ocaml
(* the pair-returning scan of the text, which is what the reader's
   [parse_program] computes:

   let rec scan pending items =
     match items with
     | [] -> ([], [])
     | item :: rest ->
       (match Ast.view item with
        | Ast.Variable lab -> (* an entry (lab, here) joins labels *)
          ...
        | _ -> (* a parsed instruction joins the code array *)
          ...)

   and [assemble] calls it as [parse_program controller] before
   handing the pair to [install_program] *)
@end example

@noindent
The book's @code{receive} argument -- the next procedure to be invoked -- is a
``continuation.''  You can consider the book's use of it as demonstrating an
elegant way to
return multiple values, or simply an excuse to show off a programming trick;
the pair the edition returns shows which parts were essential.

@code{Update-insts} modifies the instruction list, which initially contains
only the text of the instructions, to include the corresponding execution
procedures:

@example ocaml
let update_insts texts m =
  let insts = Array.map make_inst texts in
  let rec fill i =
    if i = Array.length insts
    then Ok insts
    else
      make_execution_procedure insts.(i).text m
      >>= fun exec ->
      insts.(i).exec <- exec;
      fill (i + 1)
  in
  fill 0
;;
@end example

@noindent
The machine instruction data structure simply pairs the instruction text with
the corresponding execution procedure.  The execution procedure is not yet
available when the scan constructs the instruction, and is
inserted later by @code{update-insts}.

@example ocaml
type inst =
  @{ text : instruction
  ; mutable exec : unit -> (unit, error) result @}

let make_inst text = @{ text; exec = fun () -> Ok () @}
;;
@end example

@noindent
The instruction text is not used by our simulator, but it is handy to keep
around for debugging (see @ref{Exercise 5.16} -- where it is printed --
and @ref{Exercise 5.17}, which hangs the labels beside it).  The
book's @code{instruction-execution-proc} reads the field, and
@code{set-instruction-execution-proc!} writes it; the edition's field is
simply mutable.

Elements of the label table are pairs:

@example ocaml
let make_label_entry label index = (label, index)
;;
@end example

@noindent
Entries will be looked up in the table with

@example ocaml
let lookup_label m name =
  match Hashtbl.find_opt m.labels name with
  | Some i -> Ok i
  | None -> Error (Unknown_label name)
;;
@end example

@noindent
the book's ``undefined label'' assembly error being the typed
@code{Unknown_label} failure.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.8}Exercise 5.8:} The following register-machine code
is ambiguous, because the label @code{here} is defined more than once:

@example
start
  (goto (label here))
here
  (assign a (const 3))
  (goto (label there))
here
  (assign a (const 4))
  (goto (label there))
there
@end example

With the simulator as written, what will the contents of register @code{a} be
when control reaches @code{there}?  Modify the assembler's label scan
procedure
so that the assembler will signal an error if the same label name is used to
indicate two different locations.
@end quotation

@node	5.2.3, 5.2.4, 5.2.2, 5.2
@subsection Generating Execution Procedures for Instructions

The assembler calls @code{make-execution-procedure} to generate the execution
procedure for an instruction.  Like the @code{analyze} procedure in the
evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}, this dispatches on the type of instruction to
generate the appropriate execution procedure.

@example ocaml
let make_execution_procedure inst m =
  match inst with
  | Assign _ | Assign_op _ -> make_assign inst m
  | Test _ -> make_test inst m
  | Branch _ -> make_branch inst m
  | Goto_label _ | Goto_reg _ -> make_goto inst m
  | Save _ -> make_save inst m
  | Restore _ -> make_restore inst m
  | Perform _ -> make_perform inst m
;;
@end example

@noindent
For each type of instruction in the register-machine language, there is a
generator that builds an appropriate execution procedure.  The details of these
procedures determine both the syntax and meaning of the individual instructions
in the register-machine language.  We use data abstraction to isolate the
detailed syntax of register-machine expressions from the general execution
mechanism, as we did for evaluators in @ref{4.1.2}, by using syntax
procedures to extract and classify the parts of an instruction.  In this
edition the instruction types are the constructors of the typed ADT of 5.1.5,
so the dispatch's match is exhaustive by construction: the book's
``unknown instruction type'' assembly error is a form the type system makes
unrepresentable.

@subsubheading @code{Assign} instructions

The @code{make-assign} procedure handles @code{assign} instructions:

@example ocaml
let make_assign inst m =
  let finish target value_proc =
    register_of m target
    >>= fun reg ->
    Ok
      (fun () ->
        value_proc () >>= fun v ->
        reg.contents <- v;
        advance_pc m)
  in
  match inst with
  | Assign (target, source) ->
    make_primitive_exp source m >>= fun vp -> finish target vp
  | Assign_op (target, name, inputs) ->
    make_operation_exp name inputs m >>= fun vp -> finish target (fun () -> vp () >>= apply_value_op name)
  | _ -> Error (Bad_instruction "not an assign")
;;
@end example

@noindent
@code{Make-assign} extracts the target register name and the value expression
from the @code{assign} instruction; in the typed ADT the two
@code{assign} forms -- from a single source, and from an operation applied to
its inputs -- carry these as their components, so the selectors of the text
are the components' own names:

@example ocaml
(* [Assign (target, source)] names the target register and its
   single source; [Assign_op (target, name, inputs)] names the target
   and the operation with its inputs *)
@end example

@noindent
The register name is looked up with @code{register_of} to produce the target
register object.  The value expression is passed to @code{make-operation-exp}
if the value is the result of an operation, and to @code{make-primitive-exp}
otherwise.  These procedures (shown below) parse the value expression and
produce an execution procedure for the value.  This is a procedure of no
arguments, called @code{value-proc}, which will be evaluated during the
simulation to produce the actual value to be assigned to the register.  Notice
that the work of looking up the register name and parsing the value expression
is performed just once, at assembly time, not every time the instruction is
simulated.  This saving of work is the reason we use execution procedures, and
corresponds directly to the saving in work we obtained by separating program
analysis from execution in the evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}.

The result returned by @code{make-assign} is the execution procedure for the
@code{assign} instruction.  When this procedure is called (by the machine
model's @code{execute} procedure), it sets the contents of the target register
to the result obtained by executing @code{value-proc}.  Then it advances the
@code{pc} to the next instruction by running the procedure

@example ocaml
let advance_pc m =
  incr m.pc;
  Ok ()
;;
@end example

@noindent
@code{Advance-pc} is the normal termination for all instructions except
@code{branch} and @code{goto}.

@subsubheading @code{Test}, @code{branch}, and @code{goto} instructions

@code{Make-test} handles @code{test} instructions in a similar way.  It
extracts the expression that specifies the condition to be tested and generates
an execution procedure for it.  At simulation time, the procedure for the
condition is called, the result is assigned to the @code{flag} register, and
the @code{pc} is advanced.  The typed ADT admits no non-operation test -- the
form is an operation name with its inputs -- so the book's ``bad test''
assembly error is unrepresentable; what the type cannot rule out is a test
whose operation answers something other than a boolean, and that fails at
simulation time:

@example ocaml
let make_test inst m =
  match inst with
  | Test (name, inputs) ->
    make_operation_exp name inputs m
    >>= fun cond ->
    register_of m "flag"
    >>= fun flag ->
    Ok
      (fun () ->
        cond () >>= apply_value_op name
        >>= fun v ->
        (match v with
         | Bool b ->
           flag.contents <- Bool b;
           advance_pc m
         | other ->
           Error
             (Bad_instruction
                ("the test " ^ name ^ " answered " ^ value_to_string other ^ ", not a boolean"))))
  | _ -> Error (Bad_instruction "not a test")
;;
@end example

@noindent
The execution procedure for a @code{branch} instruction checks the contents of
the @code{flag} register and either sets the contents of the @code{pc} to the
branch destination (if the branch is taken) or else just advances the @code{pc}
(if the branch is not taken).  Notice that the indicated destination in a
@code{branch} instruction must be a label, and the @code{make-branch} procedure
enforces this (the ADT's @code{Branch} constructor carries only a label name).
Notice also that the label is looked up at assembly time, not
each time the @code{branch} instruction is simulated -- the index is resolved
once, and the execution procedure closes over it.

@example ocaml
let make_branch inst m =
  match inst with
  | Branch label_name ->
    lookup_label m label_name
    >>= fun target ->
    register_of m "flag"
    >>= fun flag ->
    Ok
      (fun () ->
        (match flag.contents with
         | Bool b ->
           if b
           then (
             m.pc := target;
             Ok ())
           else advance_pc m
         | _ -> Error Branch_without_test))
  | _ -> Error (Bad_instruction "not a branch")
;;
@end example

@noindent
A @code{goto} instruction is similar to a branch, except that the destination
may be specified either as a label or as a register, and there is no condition
to check---the @code{pc} is always set to the new destination.

@example ocaml
let make_goto inst m =
  match inst with
  | Goto_label label_name ->
    lookup_label m label_name
    >>= fun target ->
    Ok
      (fun () ->
        m.pc := target;
        Ok ())
  | Goto_reg reg_name ->
    register_of m reg_name
    >>= fun reg ->
    Ok
      (fun () ->
        (match reg.contents with
         | Label l ->
           lookup_label m l >>= fun target ->
           m.pc := target;
           Ok ()
         | other ->
           Error
             (Bad_instruction ("goto reads " ^ value_to_string other ^ " from " ^ reg_name ^ ", not a label"))))
  | _ -> Error (Bad_instruction "not a goto")
;;
@end example

@subsubheading Other instructions

The stack instructions @code{save} and @code{restore} simply use the stack with
the designated register and advance the @code{pc}:

@example ocaml
let make_save inst m =
  match inst with
  | Save reg_name ->
    register_of m reg_name
    >>= fun reg ->
    Ok
      (fun () ->
        m.stack.push reg.contents;
        advance_pc m)
  | _ -> Error (Bad_instruction "not a save")
;;

let make_restore inst m =
  match inst with
  | Restore reg_name ->
    register_of m reg_name
    >>= fun reg ->
    Ok
      (fun () ->
        m.stack.pop reg.name >>= fun v ->
        reg.contents <- v;
        advance_pc m)
  | _ -> Error (Bad_instruction "not a restore")
;;
@end example

@noindent
The register's own name rides into the pop, so the underflow report names the
register the @code{restore} asked for.

The final instruction type, handled by @code{make-perform}, generates an
execution procedure for the action to be performed.  At simulation time, the
action procedure is executed and the @code{pc} advanced.

@example ocaml
let make_perform inst m =
  match inst with
  | Perform (name, inputs) ->
    make_operation_exp name inputs m
    >>= fun action ->
    Ok (fun () -> action () >>= apply_action_op name >>= fun () -> advance_pc m)
  | _ -> Error (Bad_instruction "not a perform")
;;
@end example

@subsubheading Execution procedures for subexpressions

The value of a @code{reg}, @code{label}, or @code{const} expression may be
needed for assignment to a register (@code{make-assign}) or for input to an
operation (@code{make-operation-exp}, below).  The following procedure
generates execution procedures to produce values for these expressions during
the simulation:

@example ocaml
let make_primitive_exp exp m =
  match exp with
  | Const c -> Ok (fun () -> Ok c)
  | Reg r ->
    register_of m r >>= fun reg -> Ok (fun () -> Ok reg.contents)
  | Label_source l ->
    lookup_label m l >>= fun _ -> Ok (fun () -> Ok (Label l))
;;
@end example

@noindent
The syntax of @code{reg}, @code{label}, and @code{const} expressions is
determined by the typed source ADT the shared reader produces -- its
constructors are the syntax procedures of the text, and the parse of 5.1.5 is
their grammar:

@example ocaml
type source = Sec_5_1.source =
  | Reg of string
  | Const of value
  | Label_source of string
;;
@end example

@noindent
@code{Assign}, @code{perform}, and @code{test} instructions may include the
application of a machine operation (specified by an @code{op} expression) to
some operands (specified by @code{reg} and @code{const} expressions).  The
following procedure produces an execution procedure for an ``operation
expression''---the operation name and operand expressions from the
instruction:

@example ocaml
let make_operation_exp name inputs m =
  lookup_prim name !(m.ops)
  >>= fun o ->
  let rec build = function
    | [] -> Ok []
    | e :: rest ->
      make_primitive_exp e m >>= fun p -> build rest >>= fun ps -> Ok (p :: ps)
  in
  build inputs
  >>= fun argprocs ->
  let rec collect = function
    | [] -> Ok []
    | p :: rest ->
      p () >>= fun v -> collect rest >>= fun vs -> Ok (v :: vs)
  in
  Ok (fun () -> collect argprocs >>= fun args -> Ok (o, args))
;;
@end example

@noindent
The operation expression's syntax -- the book's @code{operation-exp},
@code{operation-exp-op}, and @code{operation-exp-operands} procedures -- is
carried by the constructors themselves: the @code{Assign_op}, @code{Test}, and
@code{Perform} instructions hold the operation name and its inputs, and the
reader's parse refuses a @code{(label ...)} among the operands, a refusal
@ref{Exercise 5.9} examines.

@example ocaml
(* the operation expression is the [(op name operand ...)] form: the
   reader splits it into the name and the inputs that [Assign_op],
   [Test], and [Perform] carry *)
@end example

@noindent
Observe that the treatment of operation expressions is very much like the
treatment of procedure applications by the @code{analyze-application} procedure
in the evaluator of @ref{4.1.7} in that we generate an execution
procedure for each operand.  At simulation time, we call the operand procedures
and apply the host procedure that simulates the operation to the resulting
values.  The simulation procedure is found by looking up the operation name in
the operation table for the machine -- at assembly time, so an unknown
operation fails the assembly:

@example ocaml
let lookup_prim name ops =
  match List.assoc_opt name ops with
  | Some o -> Ok o
  | None -> Error (Unknown_operation name)
;;
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.9}Exercise 5.9:} The treatment of machine operations
above permits them to operate on labels as well as on constants and the
contents of registers.  Modify the expression-processing procedures to enforce
the condition that operations can be used only with registers and constants.
@end quotation

